{"name":"Dark Marmalade","hex":"#994939","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dark","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having an absolute or (more often) relative lack of light.","Extinguished."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A complete or (more often) partial absence of light.","Ignorance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dark"},{"term":"marmalade","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A kind of jam made with citrus fruit, distinguished by being made slightly bitter by the addition of the peel and by partial caramelisation during manufacture. Most commonly made with Seville oranges, and usually qualified by the name of the fruit when made with other types of fruit. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Quince jam."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To spread marmalade on."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/marmalade"}],"entries":[{"title":"Marmalade","blocks":[{"text":"Marmalade is a sweet, tangy fruit preserve made from the juice and peel of citrus fruits boiled with sugar and water. The well-known version is made from bitter orange, but other citrus fruits such as lemons and limes can also be used. Bitter orange is mostly used in marmalade because of its high pectin content, which gives a thick consistency to its marmalade. In addition, the balance of acid and pectin is needed for consistency. Fruits with low pectin have it added to make the marmalade."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Homemade_marmalade%2C_England.jpg/330px-Homemade_marmalade%2C_England.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marmalade"},{"title":"Darkness","blocks":[{"text":"Darkness is the condition resulting from a lack of illumination, or an absence of visible light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Creation_of_Light.png/330px-Creation_of_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darkness"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Mary_Kettilby%27s_A_Collection_of_Above_Three_Hundred_Receipts_book_%28year_1714%29_%28page_78-79%29.png/500px-Mary_Kettilby%27s_A_Collection_of_Above_Three_Hundred_Receipts_book_%28year_1714%29_%28page_78-79%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Mary_Kettilby%27s_A_Collection_of_Above_Three_Hundred_Receipts_book_%28year_1714%29_%28page_78-79%29.png/500px-Mary_Kettilby%27s_A_Collection_of_Above_Three_Hundred_Receipts_book_%28year_1714%29_%28page_78-79%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mary Kettilby's 1714 book A Collection of Above Three Hundred Receipts, pages 78–79","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMary_Kettilby's_A_Collection_of_Above_Three_Hundred_Receipts_book_(year_1714)_(page_78-79).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/FollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg/500px-FollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/FollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg/1280px-FollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/FollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg/500px-FollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Antique marmalade cutter, used to cut citrus fruit peel into thin slices","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Marmalade_spread_on_bread.jpg/500px-Marmalade_spread_on_bread.jpg?utm_source=en.wikip